# sequential-thinking MCP environment.
# run_server.bat reads this file (via load_env.bat) and launches:
#   %VENV_PATH%\Scripts\python.exe -m mcp_sequential_thinking.server
#
# SYNTAX (load_env.bat): one KEY=VALUE per line, no quotes, no spaces around '='.
# A line whose FIRST character is '#' is a comment (column 0 only). Blank lines ignored.
#
# VENV_PATH = absolute path to a Python venv (>= 3.10) that has requirements.txt installed
# plus this package itself (setup.ps1 does `pip install --upgrade <this folder>`), so no uv
# is needed at runtime. setup.ps1 writes the real .env automatically; only edit this by hand
# if you launch it manually.
VENV_PATH=C:\path_to_venv\.venv

# --- Transport ---------------------------------------------------------------
# TRANSPORT_TYPE selects how this server is exposed: stdio (default, spawned as a
# child process per client - no network) | sse | streamable-http (both listen on
# MCP_HOST:MCP_PORT and must be started as a standalone long-running process, like
# the IDA worker). MCP_HOST/MCP_PORT are only used when TRANSPORT_TYPE != stdio.
TRANSPORT_TYPE=stdio
MCP_HOST=127.0.0.1
MCP_PORT=8804

# --- Storage -----------------------------------------------------------------
# MCP_STORAGE_DIR (optional) = the storage ROOT. Each thinking session lives in its own
# spaces\<session>\ subdirectory (current_session.jsonl plus the exports/ subdirectory
# that export_session/import_session are confined to). Defaults to
# %USERPROFILE%\.mcp_sequential_thinking when unset. A store written by a pre-0.7.0
# release, sitting directly in the root, is migrated once into spaces\default\.
# MCP_STORAGE_DIR=

# --- Sessions ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Which store a call writes to is decided PER REQUEST, so one server can serve several
# targets at once without their chains, summaries and back-references mixing. Resolution
# order: the tool's `session` argument -> the X-Thinking-Session header -> ?session= on
# the URL -> MCP_DEFAULT_SESSION -> refused.
#
# SESSION_REQUIRED (default 1) refuses a call that names no session instead of quietly
# merging it into a shared "default" store - the failure this whole mechanism exists to
# prevent. Set it to 0 on a single-user server where that is just noise.
SESSION_REQUIRED=1
#
# MCP_DEFAULT_SESSION (optional) = the session to use when a call names none. Mainly for
# stdio deployments, where there is no URL or header to carry it.
# MCP_DEFAULT_SESSION=
